The database is restricted to the backend network without a published host port.

## Docker Compose Configuration

```yaml
services:
  studentportal:
    image: httpd:2.4
    container_name: students-web
    ports:
      - "8135:80"
    cpus: 0.8
    mem_limit: 448m
    restart: unless-stopped
    networks:
      - students-front
      - students-back
    privileged: false
    cap_drop:
      - ALL
    security_opt:
      - no-new-privileges:true

  database:
    image: postgres:16
    container_name: students-db
    environment:
      POSTGRES_DB: studentdb
      POSTGRES_USER: portaluser
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:?Set password}
    cpus: 0.65
    mem_limit: 640m
    restart: always
    networks:
      - students-back
    volumes:
      - students-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

networks:
  students-front:
    name: students-front
    driver: bridge

  students-back:
    name: students-back
    driver: bridge

volumes:
  students-data:
    name: students-data
```

## Security Controls

| Control | Purpose |
|---|---|
| privileged: false | Avoid privileged execution |
| cap_drop: ALL | Remove Linux capabilities |
| cap_add | Restore only justified capabilities when required |
| security_opt | Configure additional security restrictions |
| no-new-privileges | Prevent additional privilege acquisition |
| No host bind mounts | Avoid unnecessary host directory access |
| Network segmentation | Restrict unnecessary communication |
| Resource limits | Control resource consumption |
| Database port isolation | Reduce direct database exposure |

## Deployment and Verification

```powershell
docker compose config --quiet
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps
docker stats --no-stream
docker network inspect students-front
docker network inspect students-back
docker logs students-web
docker exec students-db pg_isready -U portaluser -d studentdb
```

Web service testing is performed using localhost on port 8135.

## Container Hardening Verification

```powershell
docker inspect students-web --format '{{.HostConfig.Privileged}}'
docker inspect students-web --format '{{json .HostConfig.CapDrop}}'
docker inspect students-web --format '{{json .HostConfig.CapAdd}}'
docker inspect students-web --format '{{json .HostConfig.SecurityOpt}}'
docker exec students-web cat /proc/1/status
```

| Security Parameter | Expected Configuration |
|---|---|
| Privileged Mode | false |
| Dropped Capabilities | ALL |
| Added Capabilities | Only justified capabilities if required |
| Security Option | no-new-privileges:true |
| NoNewPrivs | 1 |
| Host Bind Mounts | None |

The hardening process focuses on reducing unnecessary container permissions while maintaining service functionality.

Apache compatibility must be evaluated after removing Linux capabilities. Any capability restored through cap_add must be justified by application requirements and supported by testing.

## Persistent Storage and Recovery

| Parameter | Configuration |
|---|---|
| Database | studentdb |
| Test Table | requests |
| Original Volume | students-data |
| Backup Directory | C:\StudentsBackup |
| Backup Archive | students-data.tar.gz |
| Restored Volume | students-restored |

The recovery procedure involves stopping PostgreSQL, creating a physical volume backup, restoring the archive into a separate volume and verifying the original database records.

The final Compose configuration uses students-restored as an external volume after restoration.

